WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.932
Agentic rag is worth the extra complexity when the workflow genuinely needs to react to results.

00:00:06.932 --> 00:00:13.652
Good examples include multi-source questions, vague user wording, missing evidence, conditional tool use,

00:00:13.652 --> 00:00:19.436
or situations where the first retrieval often needs correction.

00:00:20.126 --> 00:00:28.107
It is probably unnecessary when one stable knowledge base answers most questions reliably with one retrieval pass.

00:00:28.107 --> 00:00:34.348
A simple frequently asked questions assistant may work very well with classic rag.

00:00:34.348 --> 00:00:43.803
Before adding an agent, ask one design question: does the system need to inspect a result before choosing the next step?

00:00:43.803 --> 00:00:48.358
If the answer is no, a fixed pipeline is probably simpler.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:56.182
Remember that every extra decision adds latency, cost, testing work, and more possible failure modes.

00:00:56.182 --> 00:00:58.869
Complexity should solve a real problem.
